WEBVTT

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:03.610
Our concerns about our prisons falling on deaf ears.

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:11.450
And the Home Secretary condemns Donald Trump's ban on people from some majority Muslim countries entering the United States.

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:13.910
It is divisive and wrong.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:17.280
But let's begin with the big Brexit debate.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:23.900
MPs have held the first day of discussion on the bill, formally starting the process of the UK exiting the EU.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.780
They'll finish their debate and vote on Wednesday.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.980
The government was forced to bring in the bill after...

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.020
a case in the Supreme Court where the government's intention that ministers

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:38.760
alone should be able to start the process was challenged by campaigners who argued

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:43.680
it should be a decision for Parliament. The SNP and the Liberal Democrats are

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:48.100
opposing the legislation but it is expected to pass because Labour MPs

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:51.890
have been ordered to support it. The Secretary of State set out the

00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:56.320
government's case. It's not a bill about whether the UK should leave the

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.320
European Union, or indeed how it should do so.

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:08.250
It is simply about Parliament empowering the government to implement a decision already made, a point of no return already passed.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:13.710
We asked the people of the UK if they wanted to leave the European Union. They decided they did.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:22.700
So at the core of this bill lies a very simple question. Do we trust the people or not?

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:29.360
The aim is to take this opportunity for the United Kingdom to emerge from this period of change stronger.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:34.000
fairer, more united and more outward-looking than ever before.

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:38.690
We have been clear that there must be no attempts to remain inside the European Union,

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:43.460
no attempts to rejoin it through the back door, and no second referendum.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:48.140
The eyes of the nation are on this chamber as we consider this bill.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:52.470
For many years there's been a creeping sense in the country, and not just this country,

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:55.620
that politicians say one thing and do another.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:59.920
We voted to give the people the chance to determine our future in a referendum.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:05.280
Now we must honour our side of the agreement to vote to deliver on the result.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.680
So really, we are considering that very simple question.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.160
Do we trust the people or not?

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.880
For generations, my party has done so.

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:17.380
Now that question is before every member of this House.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:20.840
This bill provides the power for the Prime Minister to begin that process

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.980
and honour the decision made by the people of the United Kingdom

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:25.700
on the 23rd of June last year.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:28.340
I commend it to the House. Trust the people.

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:33.570
His Labour shadow was in reflective mood.

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:35.430
We lost the referendum.

00:02:37.190 --> 00:02:38.790
Yes, the result was close.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:42.180
Yes, there were lies and half-truths,

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:46.500
none worse than the false promise of £350 million a week for the NHS.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:56.620
Yes, yes, technically the referendum is not legally binding,

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.100
but the result was not technical.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:08.460
It was deeply political, and politically the notion that the referendum was merely a consultation exercise to inform Parliament holds no water.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:21.030
I hope the respectful approach that I've tried to adopt to colleagues and to the anxiety among the 48% is reflected across the House,

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:29.540
and that we will see a good deal less of the gloating from those who campaign to leave than we've seen in the past.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:38.160
It is our duty to accept and respect the outcome of the referendum but we remain a European country

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:46.420
with a shared history and shared values and it is also our duty to fight for a new relationship

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:51.850
with our EU partners that reflects our values our commitment to internationalism and our

00:03:51.850 --> 00:03:57.540
commitment to an open and tolerant society. Above all it is our duty to ensure an outcome

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:59.700
that is not just for the 52%.

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:05.160
or the 48%, but for the 100%, that we will do.

00:04:06.140 --> 00:04:12.240
Well, a veteran Conservative pro-European said he'd be voting for an SNP amendment,

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:17.160
attempting to stop the bill in its tracks, and addressed criticism of him for doing so.

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:23.500
Ken Clarke wondered what his party's Eurosceptics would have done had they been on the losing side.

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:29.980
I don't say that as criticism. I actually am on good terms with the hardline Eurosceptics because I respect...

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:36.060
their sincerity and the passionate nature of their beliefs, but if I ever live to see

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:42.860
the Honourable Member for Stone turn up here and vote in favour of Britain remaining in

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:51.340
the European Union, well, I retract what I say, but a referendum... Hot tongs would

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:57.220
not make the Honourable Member for Stone vote for membership in the European Union.

00:04:57.820 --> 00:04:58.900
He turned to trade.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:04:59.960
Apparently, you f-----

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:07.050
follow the rabbit down the hole and you emerge in a wonderland where suddenly countries throughout

00:05:07.050 --> 00:05:14.030
the world are queuing up to give us trading advantages and access to their markets that

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:21.320
previously we've never been able to achieve as part of the European Union. Nice men like

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:29.180
President Trump, President Erdogan are just impatient to abandon their normal protectionism

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:29.960
and give it.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:38.860
Don't let me be too cynical. I hope that's right. I do want the best outcome for the United

00:05:38.860 --> 00:05:46.620
Kingdom from this process. No doubt somewhere there's a hatter holding a tea party with

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:57.820
a door mouse in the teeth. We need success in these trade negotiations to recoup at least

00:05:57.820 --> 00:05:59.680
some of the losses

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:05.080
which we're going to incur from leaving the single market.

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:06.560
This is a big deal.

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:10.760
You're not just divvying up the Nanamoscuri records here

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:13.120
or divvying up the Borgen box set.

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:16.740
This has an impact on each and every one of us.

00:06:17.060 --> 00:06:19.280
Europe is where our future lies.

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:23.340
It's one where we tackle inequality, climate change,

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:25.560
refugees get help,

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:29.160
areas that don't get much of a hearing in Whitehall these days,

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:29.920
pooling.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.480
our sovereignty and working together is a good thing. Passing this bill and turning

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:41.260
your back on our amendment would turn its back on the progress made and disrespect the

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:47.230
devolution settlement. I would urge members to vote for our amendment. Otherwise, this

00:06:47.230 --> 00:06:53.970
is a backward and damaging step, and it is an act of constitutional and economic sabotage.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:59.970
The British people gave the government the mandate to pull the United Kingdom out of

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.860
the European Union. No, I want to make some progress. The British people did not give

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:10.850
this Government the mandate to threaten to turn our country into some tawdry, low-regulation,

00:07:10.950 --> 00:07:18.360
low-tax cowboy economy. The British people did not vote to make themselves poorer by

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:24.180
pulling ourselves out of the greatest free-trading single market the world has ever seen. Incidentally,

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:28.080
that is one of the many reasons why the Liberal Democrats believe the British people should

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:29.780
be given the say at the end of the process.

00:07:30.090 --> 00:07:34.890
much as they were given at the beginning, and the British people most certainly did not give

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:40.430
the mandate to the government to indulge in this ludicrous sycophantic farce we have seen

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:46.110
in recent days in which this government, having burnt every bridge left with our friends in

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:51.890
Europe, rushes across the Atlantic to sidle next to a US president who they do not seem

00:07:51.890 --> 00:07:58.430
to be aware of, whose nativism, whose isolationism, whose protectionism is diametrically opposed

00:07:58.430 --> 00:07:59.710
to the long term strategic

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.190
interests of the United Kingdom. There are a number of excuses that have been made. We

00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:11.110
heard it from the former leader of the Liberal Democrats, that people did not know what they

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:17.210
were voting for. Well, first of all, there is no excuse for people in this House to

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:22.460
not know what they are voting for now, because the Prime Minister has made it very clear in

00:08:22.460 --> 00:08:25.980
6,000 words, this is what we are voting for.

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:35.350
And during the referendum campaign, the people of the United Kingdom knew what they were voting for.

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:43.870
In fact, those who wanted the vote to remain tried to scare the devil out of them when it came to the vote.

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:48.450
They told them all kinds of horrors were going to beset them.

00:08:48.590 --> 00:08:56.530
That within a couple of days, they were going to be eating bread and having to drink water and lose their jobs.

00:08:56.750 --> 00:08:59.240
And still they voted to leave.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:06.700
The DUP's Sammy Wilson, and we'll have more from that debate a little later in the program.

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:12.460
Now to other news, and the Home Secretary Amber Rudd has said that the US travel ban

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:17.080
creates a potential propaganda opportunity for so-called Islamic State.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:21.880
Appearing before the Home Affairs Committee, she also said officials were working to take

00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:24.080
down extremist online postings.

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:29.720
Donald Trump has signed an executive order suspending refugee resettlement.

00:09:30.030 --> 00:09:35.210
and blocking individuals from seven majority Muslim nations from entering the United States.

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:38.620
Do you disagree with the Trump ban?

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:43.160
Yes, and I support the position that the government has taken,

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:47.780
the Foreign Secretary spelt out that it is divisive and wrong.

00:09:48.220 --> 00:09:56.340
We've obviously had reported in the last few days ISIL supporters being monitored on social media,

00:09:56.620 --> 00:09:59.980
on things like Telegram messaging service, saying things like this.

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:06.120
is a blessed ban because it helps them to increase recruitment. There's a lot of people

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:14.380
talking about Anwar al-Awlaki and the al-Qaeda leader who claimed that the West would eventually

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:21.660
turn against its Muslim citizens and using this as a way to say that he was right and

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:27.940
also reports of former security officers and agents warning that this is going to make

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:29.740
it harder to combat.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.360
extremism? Are you worried about this?

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:34.280
Well, I mean, ISIL and Daesh will use

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:35.800
any opportunity they can

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:37.880
to make difficulties to create

00:10:37.880 --> 00:10:40.540
the environment that they want, to radicalise

00:10:40.540 --> 00:10:42.360
people, to bring them over to their

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:44.280
side. So it is a propaganda opportunity

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:45.520
for them, potentially.

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:48.420
At the moment, what we will do is continue to monitor

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:49.940
what is said, continue to

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:52.340
take down the sort of literature

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:54.420
and postings that we see

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.260
on the internet that try to

00:10:56.260 --> 00:10:58.360
encourage that sort of extremism. They may

00:10:58.360 --> 00:10:59.540
use this as

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.260
example and we will continue to monitor and take down the sites where we can.

00:11:04.700 --> 00:11:08.860
Another Labour MP turned to the decision to invite President Trump on a state

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:16.010
visit. But why spend this money policing an individual with all the added costs

00:11:16.010 --> 00:11:22.960
that come through a state visit, an individual who has peddled hate, racism,

00:11:24.590 --> 00:11:29.760
misogyny, Islamophobia, where many people in this country would rather that money

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:29.980
But

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:33.280
deployed on actually, for example, policing our communities?

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:39.630
Well, I mean, he is the head of state of the USA. We have an arrangement internationally

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:45.340
where we look after each other's head of states. If the Queen goes abroad, we expect other

00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:50.200
countries to look after her, to spend money. We think that she is universally popular.

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:54.870
There may be a few countries that might not take that view, but they still look after

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:56.770
her and spend money on policing her.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.740
watching Tuesday in Parliament with me, Alessia McCarthy.

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:12.000
The Chief Inspector of Prisons for England and Wales, Peter Clarke, says many of his reports

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:17.500
are falling on deaf ears. He told the Justice Committee that jails which did not put his

00:12:17.500 --> 00:12:22.220
recommendations into practice often failed to improve or even went into decline.

00:12:22.780 --> 00:12:29.300
Both of you are hugely experienced in the criminal justice system with an enormous amount of

00:12:29.300 --> 00:12:29.940
knowledge is done.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:36.050
how it works. So Mr. Clarke, starting with you in 60 seconds, tell me what's wrong with

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:36.510
our prisons?

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:42.440
Basically, they're unsafe, they're full of drugs, we have an ageing population, the physical

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:47.160
environment is appalling, and there are far too many people in our prisons who are suffering

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:52.780
from mental health issues. And in my judgment, those five issues will create a major obstruction

00:12:52.780 --> 00:12:54.140
to the reform programme.

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:59.780
Mr. Clarke, so who's holding up this reform? You've identified the problems, you have...

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:01.740
talked about what needs to be improved.

00:13:02.270 --> 00:13:07.410
Where is the block in the system that prevents the changes that needs to be made?

00:13:08.780 --> 00:13:11.140
Well, in terms of those issues which I've mentioned,

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:14.580
they're issues which we frequently refer to in our reports.

00:13:14.780 --> 00:13:19.910
And if I have a frustration, it is that far too often our reports,

00:13:19.910 --> 00:13:22.720
it feels as if they're falling on deaf ears.

00:13:23.320 --> 00:13:26.230
And not in all prisons, but in some.

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:29.980
And typically, the prisons which do not...

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:34.940
respond positively to our reports where there's a hugely low uptake or

00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:39.840
implementation of our recommendations are those prisons which do not improve

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:44.740
or which actually decline in the way in which they treat prisoners and the

00:13:44.740 --> 00:13:49.770
outcomes that prisoners encounter in those prisons. Over the past year there

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:55.110
has been a decline in the number of our recommendations overall that have been

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:59.310
implemented and most worryingly a decline in the area of the

00:13:59.310 --> 00:13:59.670
recommendation.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:03.140
in the area of safety, and in fact we're now at the position

00:14:03.140 --> 00:14:07.040
where more of our recommendations in the subject of safety

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:09.660
are not being achieved than are being achieved.

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:12.340
Just let me ask you a question then about the opportunities

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:14.320
for the views of prisoners to be heard.

00:14:15.210 --> 00:14:17.790
Do you think those are sufficient at the moment?

00:14:18.700 --> 00:14:24.130
Even if I were to believe just some of the stories I receive in my postbag,

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:29.810
then in some places there are clearly blockages.

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:36.690
and I've seen this on several inspections where I quite often don't get mobbed on a wing

00:14:36.690 --> 00:14:39.510
but I get approached by significant numbers of prisoners

00:14:39.510 --> 00:14:44.760
and very often their complaint is about mundane day-to-day issues

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:52.000
getting equipment, getting supplies, getting some bedsheets, getting some clothing,

00:14:52.660 --> 00:14:54.920
getting their canteen and so on and so forth.

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:59.980
It's low-level stuff which has an enormous impact on how a prisoner...

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.660
feels about their day-to-day existence.

00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:07.130
The government has denied reports that it's planning to nationalise the franchise, which

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:10.930
includes Southern Railway, after months of strikes and disruption.

00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:16.430
The transport minister told Piers there were no plans to strip Govia-Thameslink Railway,

00:15:16.450 --> 00:15:18.990
also known as GTR, of its franchise.

00:15:19.910 --> 00:15:24.510
His comments followed reports that ministers were considering taking direct control, with

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:26.570
officials preparing a number of options.

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:29.980
MALCOLM BRABANT, President of the United States of Africa, the United States of England

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:34.300
Ovia-Themstink railway of their franchise. The speculation in the media is just that,

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:39.560
speculation. DFT, of course, continue to monitor the operational and contractual performance of

00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:46.020
all franchisees. Could the government say what benefits current Southern Rail passengers have

00:15:46.020 --> 00:15:50.620
gained from a private operator running their railway service under the present franchise

00:15:50.620 --> 00:15:56.010
agreement in view of the extent of the widely recognised poor performance from that train

00:15:56.010 --> 00:15:59.890
operator over the last two years not related to induction.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:08.380
We are acutely aware of the challenges which all people who are using that particular franchise

00:16:08.380 --> 00:16:13.950
are currently facing, but the noble Lord tried to distinguish between the industrial dispute

00:16:13.950 --> 00:16:18.930
which has, as I have always said from this dispatch box, compounded the challenges that

00:16:18.930 --> 00:16:23.710
network rail is facing and the government has given a commitment, an extra £300 million

00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:27.030
in investment on the Brighton main line.

00:16:27.030 --> 00:16:29.150
and in terms of the industrial dispute itself.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:36.280
contextualise this as I've done before. RMT are out on dispute on a new contract. Every single

00:16:36.500 --> 00:16:43.060
supervisor, train supervisor as they've now called, has signed that contract. Everyone, not a single

00:16:43.060 --> 00:16:48.260
one, is exempted. They are working on the new contract. There are no job losses on the new

00:16:48.260 --> 00:16:53.200
contract. There is no pay cut on those new job losses and what's more, they're guaranteed a

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:59.740
job until 2021. Even I can't lay claim to that. And staying with trains, an 11th hour bid to

00:16:59.740 --> 00:16:59.900
You did.

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:03.640
rail, the high-speed rail project has failed in the House of Lords.

00:17:04.420 --> 00:17:10.860
Peers opposed a backbench move to block legislation that paves the way for the scheme by 386 votes

00:17:10.860 --> 00:17:17.690
to 26. The long-awaited HS2 project is now set to go ahead after more than three years

00:17:17.690 --> 00:17:22.960
of parliamentary scrutiny. A bill aimed at increasing the number of prosecutions after

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:26.980
so-called honour crimes has gained initial approval in the House of Commons.

00:17:27.200 --> 00:17:29.980
The bill put forward by the Conservative, Nusrat,

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.740
Sirat Ghani, would ban the use of the phrase honour killings from all official publications.

00:17:35.830 --> 00:17:40.350
She said the police were often reluctant to deal with domestic violence in ethnic communities

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:46.080
for fear of racism, and she cited the experience of a young woman abused throughout her marriage.

00:17:46.860 --> 00:17:52.560
Sabjit was terrorised, going to bed, not knowing if she would be alive the next day.

00:17:53.480 --> 00:17:57.820
She was told that the honour of her family was at stake if she complained,

00:17:58.040 --> 00:17:59.980
and that police would just treat her...

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:07.350
as a number. Sarbjic told me that she didn't feel alive, but nor was she dead. When she

00:18:07.350 --> 00:18:13.030
did sum up the courage, she called Crimestoppers as well as the police. She risked her life

00:18:13.030 --> 00:18:19.900
in reaching out. But after statements were taken, she was returned home to her abusers

00:18:19.900 --> 00:18:23.540
because it was just a cultural misunderstanding.

00:18:24.500 --> 00:18:27.500
But her bill was opposed by a fellow Conservative.

00:18:27.500 --> 00:18:29.960
But I believe that the discriminatory...

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:36.060
premise of this bill is wrong, because not all victims are female and not all offenders

00:18:36.060 --> 00:18:42.100
are male. We should be bringing forward gender-neutral legislation that seeks to help all victims

00:18:42.100 --> 00:18:47.980
of crime, whether they be men or women, and to punish all offenders of these crimes, whether

00:18:47.980 --> 00:18:53.150
they be men or women. People say she says that. We are looking at the screen, Mr. Speaker.

00:18:53.530 --> 00:18:59.080
It says crime, aggravated murder of and violence against women. It does not mention men. It

00:18:59.080 --> 00:18:59.800
It's no good say anything.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:06.960
It doesn't include it. It's there on the screen for honourable members if they can't read or hear what's actually happening.

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:10.220
They clearly haven't read the bill that's being brought forward.

00:19:12.020 --> 00:19:17.180
But despite Mr Davies' objections, MPs allowed Nusrat Ghani's bill to make progress,

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:20.500
although it stands no realistic chance of becoming law.

00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:26.620
For the main business, of course, was the EU exit debate, to which we'll return now,

00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:29.480
with passionate contributions from all sides of the House.

00:19:30.250 --> 00:19:34.810
So many were keen to be heard that the Speaker had to make this plea for forbearance.

00:19:35.400 --> 00:19:44.300
Can I appeal to members, please, not to keep coming up to the chair and asking where they are on the list.

00:19:44.730 --> 00:19:52.080
Not doing so explicitly and not doing so by the back door, by asking, is it all right if I go to the loo?

00:19:52.120 --> 00:19:55.060
May I have a cup of tea? Am I permitted to eat a biscuit?

00:19:55.320 --> 00:19:59.960
Look, I'm going to do my best to accommodate everybody in the substantial time...

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:05.000
available, but I appeal to colleagues to show a little bit of patience and some

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:08.340
regard for the chair trying to concentrate on the debate. I'll get you

00:20:08.340 --> 00:20:09.880
in if I possibly can.

00:20:10.300 --> 00:20:12.380
For me, this referendum...

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:15.680
was a massive peaceful revolution by consent.

00:20:16.090 --> 00:20:17.690
of historic proportions.

00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:19.640
This bill.

00:20:20.600 --> 00:20:23.380
at last endorses that revolution.

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:25.850
From the 17th century.

00:20:26.680 --> 00:20:27.720
right the way through.

00:20:28.340 --> 00:20:28.720
Oh!

00:20:29.110 --> 00:20:29.590
history

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:32.250
through the Corn Laws.

00:20:32.940 --> 00:20:36.660
through the parliamentary reformat which gave the vote to the working class.

00:20:37.630 --> 00:20:38.430
The suffragettes.

00:20:38.600 --> 00:20:40.180
who got the vote in 928.

00:20:41.060 --> 00:20:43.380
and then again in the period of appeasement.

00:20:43.980 --> 00:20:47.740
These have all been great benchmarks of British history.

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:54.840
And they have all ultimately been determined by the decisions that have been taken in this House.

00:20:55.500 --> 00:20:57.740
And if I may be permitted to say so,

00:20:58.890 --> 00:20:59.290
Bye.

00:20:59.630 --> 00:20:59.930
back.

00:21:00.180 --> 00:21:00.900
Benchers.

00:21:01.080 --> 00:21:02.340
The referendum.

00:21:03.350 --> 00:21:04.550
decided only one thing.

00:21:05.270 --> 00:21:07.910
the fact that we are leaving the institutions of the European Union.

00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:08.760
Mm-hmm.

00:21:08.790 --> 00:21:11.930
It did not determine the terms on which we leave.

00:21:12.440 --> 00:21:13.560
It did not decide.

00:21:14.010 --> 00:21:17.130
the new relationship we will have with the other 27 member states.

00:21:17.430 --> 00:21:20.430
And that is why we have as a nation to get our objectives right

00:21:20.430 --> 00:21:24.370
and the process right as we start this great negotiation.

00:21:25.240 --> 00:21:27.020
And I have to say, the government's handling.

00:21:27.580 --> 00:21:29.920
of this thus far has not shown.

00:21:30.090 --> 00:21:31.830
sufficient respect for Parliament.

00:21:32.900 --> 00:21:36.160
Notwithstanding the number of times the Secretary of State has come to the dispatch box.

00:21:36.680 --> 00:21:40.040
Because for several months, Ministers appeared to believe that saying,

00:21:40.260 --> 00:21:41.880
well, we're not going to give you a running commentary,

00:21:41.940 --> 00:21:45.680
and if you ask for greater clarity, in the words of the No. 10 spokesperson,

00:21:45.980 --> 00:21:49.860
you are not backing the UK team, was the right way to approach it.

00:21:50.000 --> 00:21:50.800
It wasn't.

00:21:51.270 --> 00:21:55.050
One Labour MP suggested to the Leave campaigner Ian Duncan-Smith

00:21:55.050 --> 00:21:58.730
that there hadn't been enough information published about this bill.

00:22:00.000 --> 00:22:04.780
40 years, if anybody in this House doesn't have enough information to make a decision

00:22:04.780 --> 00:22:08.640
about this, I wonder where they've been in the last 40 years or the years that they've

00:22:08.640 --> 00:22:09.960
spent in here. Of course we have enough.

00:22:10.810 --> 00:22:13.990
information. The question that she is referring to is the publication of the white paper.

00:22:14.290 --> 00:22:17.690
The Government has said they are going to publish the white paper. I stand by that.

00:22:17.790 --> 00:22:21.290
I think that is a very good idea, although I must say that my right hon. Friend, the Prime

00:22:21.290 --> 00:22:25.070
Minister, made a pretty good fist of it in her recent speech, in which she set out 12

00:22:25.070 --> 00:22:29.610
points that would guide her negotiation. I do hope the Government reprints that.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:34.240
with a couple of diagrams, the odd explanation and a nice picture and I think that will make

00:22:34.240 --> 00:22:35.320
an excellent white paper.

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:40.820
I am sure that the Secretary of State, who like me, has had a degree of experience in

00:22:40.820 --> 00:22:42.560
complex international negotiations.

00:22:43.370 --> 00:22:44.810
is as conscious as I am.

00:22:45.350 --> 00:22:48.250
that one of the first prerequisites is to listen.

00:22:48.650 --> 00:22:49.510
to the words.

00:22:50.570 --> 00:22:51.370
It was not.

00:22:51.720 --> 00:22:53.080
President of the United States.

00:22:53.550 --> 00:22:55.710
who said that Britain would be at the front of the queue.

00:22:55.990 --> 00:22:57.530
It was British politicians.

00:22:58.250 --> 00:22:58.470
Yeah.

00:22:58.660 --> 00:22:59.960
What the president said was,

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:01.240
you're doing great.

00:23:02.700 --> 00:23:04.840
I don't take as much comfort.

00:23:07.260 --> 00:23:11.080
The other area that I just want to raise is the question of, again,

00:23:11.830 --> 00:23:19.970
This idea that somehow if you voted to leave, you were, if not an outright racist, an indirect racist.

00:23:20.310 --> 00:23:29.990
And that has been so ridiculous and so appalling that people, the 17 million people who voted to leave, have been treated in that way.

00:23:30.020 --> 00:23:37.040
And we know that what people voted for was not against immigrants, but against the idea

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:41.940
that 27 other countries, 26 excluding the Republic of Ireland, could come into our

00:23:41.940 --> 00:23:49.740
country without any reason other than that they could come, whereas outside the European

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:53.840
Union, and we betrayed the Commonwealth so badly back in 1973.

00:23:53.840 --> 00:23:59.140
You can't go back on your word because you don't agree with the result.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:04.060
But I want to say this, I believe history will not be kind.

00:24:04.600 --> 00:24:09.200
to this Parliament, nor indeed the Government I was so proud to serve in.

00:24:09.720 --> 00:24:14.600
How on earth did we ever come to put an alternative to the people, which we then said?

00:24:14.970 --> 00:24:16.310
Would make them worse off.

00:24:16.860 --> 00:24:17.680
Less safe.

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:19.780
and would weaken our nation.

00:24:20.730 --> 00:24:22.350
I greatly fear and echoing.

00:24:22.740 --> 00:24:23.900
The wise words of

00:24:24.090 --> 00:24:24.610
Some.

00:24:24.950 --> 00:24:29.410
of the speech from my honourable new friend, the member for Sheffield Hallow.

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:39.220
that I greatly fear generations who either didn't vote or are yet to come will not thank us for our great following.

00:24:39.680 --> 00:24:41.580
And that's it from me for now.

00:24:41.680 --> 00:24:44.960
But do join me on Wednesday night at 11 for the conclusion of the...

00:24:45.000 --> 00:24:52.070
debate and the vote on the bill to begin our exit from the EU. But for now from me, Alicia McCarthy, goodbye.
